          "type": "turn",
          "question": "If a record says \"diabetes with neuropathy\", does the coder need a statement that the diabetes caused the neuropathy?",
          "answer": "No, the coder does not need a separate statement that the diabetes caused the neuropathy, based on the coding guidance I could retrieve.\n\nThe ICD-10-CM conventions document says that when the classification's Alphabetic Index links two conditions with the word \"with\", it reads that as \"associated with\" or \"due to\" and presumes the relationship. The record does not have to state it. The same convention appears in the guidance on coding type 2 diabetes (E11). Diabetic neuropathy is coded from the diabetes category, not the polyneuropathy block, because the classification presumes the association.\n\nThe presumption has limits. It applies only to the pairings the index actually lists. It is overridden if the documentation says the two conditions are unrelated. The diabetes guidance also notes that a condition appearing on the same problem list is not automatically a diabetic complication.\n\nThe retrieved passages did not cover every case. They do not say how to handle a record where the neuropathy is described in a way that doesn't match the index entry. For that, a certified coder or the official coding guidelines would be the right source.\n\nSources: ICD-10-CM code structure and conventions; Coding type 2 diabetes: E11; ICD-10-CM Chapter 6: nervous system.\n\nNot medical advice. This is an educational software project. It does not diagnose or treat, and it must not be used for real medical decisions.",
